items.concept:
The 'bag of dicts' disease: data code where every function takes and returns dicts. Symptoms: quote['tikcer'] typos crash at runtime three layers deep, nobody knows which keys exist without reading five functions, autocomplete is useless, and refactors are archaeology.

@dataclass cures it for internal data: declared fields with types, defaults and default_factory for mutable defaults, frozen=True for values that must not change after creation, and free __init__/__repr__/__eq__. With slots=True you also get lower memory and a guard against typo'd attributes.

The boundary rule you'll use all quarter: **Pydantic at the edges, dataclasses inside**. Pydantic validates untrusted input (API payloads, LLM output, files) at the boundary; once data is inside your system it is already trusted, and a plain dataclass is faster and dependency-free. Same instinct as airport security: check passports at the border, not at every coffee shop.

